## Introduction 

Ross Goodwin's 1 the Road is an experimental novel generated by an artificial intelligence system during a road trip from New York to New Orleans in March 2017. This analysis compares three approaches to geocoding the locations mentioned in the novel: manual annotation, automated geocoding using spaCy and geopy, and automated geocoding using a large language model (LLM). By examining their spatial distributions, similarities and discrepancies, this comparison explores the extent to which automated systems can accurately represent geographical space and the importance of human interpretation when working with ambiguous or incomplete textual data.

## Comparing the Spatial Distribution of Locations 

When comparing the three maps, one noticeable pattern is that all methods identify several of the major cities associated with the journey. However, they differ in the distribution and accuracy of the locations they produce.

The manually geocoded map shows a relatively concentrated distribution of locations around cities along the journey, including Philadelphia, Baltimore, Washington, Columbia, Biloxi and New Orleans. New Orleans, the destination, has the greatest concentration of marked locations. However, an interesting thing to point out is that New York, where the journey begins, does not appear to have any geocoded locations. There are also noticeable gaps between certain cities, particularly between Washington and Raleigh. These gaps may partly reflect the incomplete nature of the text available for annotation, as some sections of the original novel may have been removed. This means that when the novel doesn't clearly mention a place, it's harder for a human annotator to identify and mark it on the map.

In comparison, the spaCy and geopy method produces a larger number of geocoded locations, creating a more continuous representation of the journey. The automated method identified more geographical references than the manual method. This could be because it picked up locations that human annotators either overlooked or deliberately excluded because they did not appear relevant to the journey.


